WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:07.980
DAN AHARON: Speech at Google--

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.550
it's been a long journey.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:18.150
So we originally developed
voice search for Google Search--

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.290
I think it was
over 15 years ago.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:24.600
And then we kind of
took that technology

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.750
and brought it to other
products like Google Maps--

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.720
for voice search in
maps for navigation--

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.450
and text-to-speech
for navigation.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:38.640
And captions in YouTube and
Google Assistant-- and we

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.790
invested all that effort
into building a robust speech

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.580
engine that can work across
a large number of languages

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.730
and doesn't need as much
tuning as technology

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.810
that existed at the time all
based on machine learning.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.628
And then we took that
technology-- three years ago--

00:00:54.628 --> 00:00:56.670
and we brought it to the
cloud for the first time

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.075
with Cloud Speech-to-Text as
part of our developer products.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:04.280
Oh, can we go back to a slide?

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:09.790
Yeah, it's OK.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.790
So speech-to-text and
then text-to-speech--

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.710
they're our developer products.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:19.930
So you can use them to
power all the applications

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:24.260
you build with AI technology.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:28.720
And then last year, we
introduced our business

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.160
solutions--

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.640
which are higher-level
of abstraction,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.760
which include Dialogflo
and Contact Center AI.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.280
There's going to be a
session about those tomorrow.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.040
So you're welcome
to check it out.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.510
They take some of
the technologies

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.550
we expose in speech-to-text
and text-to-speech

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:49.180
and apply them to
specific use cases

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.762
like chat bots and voice
bots and the Contact Center.

00:01:52.762 --> 00:01:54.220
So you can use them
in more places.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.780
So as I mentioned, we
have two products in Cloud

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.710
with the raw speech
capabilities that

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:09.229
are leveraged everywhere else.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:14.690
Speech-to-Text is available
in 120 languages--

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:18.530
so, far more than any
other speech service

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.690
that's available today.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.420
And it provides service
in real-time streaming

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.700
as well as batch.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.950
One of the things that is
special-- it performs really

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.610
well in noisy environments.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.910
We'll see if I'll regret
saying that when we actually

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.340
do some demos later.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.100
But typically-- it's trained
on a lot of noisy data.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.230
So it performs well,
even if there's

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:43.340
traffic in the background, or
people speaking, or whatever.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.460
And Text-to-Speech, it's
the only production service

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.110
out there with WaveNet.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.268
We'll see later why
that actually matters.

00:02:51.268 --> 00:02:53.060
It's available in 21
languages, but there's

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.310
going to be more coming
later in text and SSML.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.730
So since we first came
out with Speech-to-Text,

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:05.670
there's been a lot of new
development in this space.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.770
As you can see here,
we took it to GA.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:11.140
We improved accuracy.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.520
We improved the speed.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.330
And then we introduced a bunch
of new features like language

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:22.490
auto-detect, word-level
confidence, timestamps--

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:26.690
then in February,
two months ago,

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.480
we came out with a
bunch of new launches.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.810
And I'm going to talk a little
bit more about them next.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:39.110
So we wanted to make our speech
products more accessible.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.860
And we're doing
that in three ways.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:44.310
One is we're making
the premium models that

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.020
were only available
to select groups

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.890
now available to everyone.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:53.280
Two, we're reducing prices
on some of our products

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.680
by up to 50%.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.000
And three, we're increasing
language support, especially

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.433
for Text-to-Speech.

00:04:03.433 --> 00:04:05.850
So a little bit on the premium
models-- so as I mentioned,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:09.660
the core speech-recognition
technology in Cloud

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.460
was based on the
technology that Google

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.650
developed for other products.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:18.750
But what we quickly found out
is the Cloud developers weren't

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.610
just applying them
on the same use cases

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.770
that Google Search,
or Google Assistant,

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.720
or the Google products
used, but actually

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.290
for a lot of enterprise
applications--

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.340
so like transcribing
phone calls,

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:33.870
for IVRs, or for
indexing videos,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.390
or adding subtitles to
videos, or transcribing

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.810
meetings, or all of
these other use cases.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.730
And so we invested
specific effort

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.980
for improving
these cloud models.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.170
And this resulted in
our premium models

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.830
that were launched in beta--

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:50.640
I think it was--

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.840
April last year-- so
exactly one year ago.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:01.320
So these premium models improved
performance by 64% and 62%.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.160
So this is the percentage
of reduction in error.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.510
So you could argue it's 2x to 3x
better than what existed before

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.220
for these specific
mediums because there

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.710
was a concerted
effort on these areas.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:20.100
And then when they launched,
and [INAUDIBLE] phone_call model

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.690
was based on data from opt-in
customers in Cloud that

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.420
gave us the permission use their
data to improve the models.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.760
So we only made it available
for others that also contributed

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.135
for that program.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.420
And now, we're sort of
opening it up further.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.880
So I'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.860
But first, let's
switch to the demo.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.660
I can show you guys--

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:50.780
we get a lot of questions
from people about.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.230
Oh, I think it's showing
the wrong screen.

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.110
Do I need to change something?

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:58.722
Actually, maybe I drag it?

00:05:58.722 --> 00:06:00.485
KEVIN NORTHOVER:
That's the other demo.

00:06:00.485 --> 00:06:02.970
DAN AHARON: No,
that's the wrong one.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.720
Switch back?

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:11.030
Yeah, maybe if I drag
my screen over here?

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:11.660
Does that work?

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:21.140
Sorry, guys.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:31.790
Let me change my display
settings so that it's mirrored.

00:06:38.538 --> 00:06:39.530
Mirror displays.

00:06:43.994 --> 00:06:46.020
OK, I think it's showing now.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:58.930
So a lot of people ask us--
they tell us we already

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:00.190
use speech recognition.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:03.080
And we know that Google
speech recognition is better.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:06.730
But what we have is
good enough for us.

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:08.712
What's happening is
speech recognition

00:07:08.712 --> 00:07:10.420
is used in more and
more use cases today.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:13.730
And the bar is actually
getting higher.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.460
So you can see here--
this is an example

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:17.500
of a conversation
with a voice bot

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:20.060
where you're getting
100% accuracy.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.490
And you can see--

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:25.480
you can build a very
delightful experience

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:28.870
for users when everything
is working properly, right.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:32.690
So this is an example of someone
ordering a burger and the voice

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:33.550
bot responding.

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.130
And everything works great.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:39.370
But then if you go down to
a lower level of accuracy,

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:40.660
even at 90%--

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:44.140
which for harsh
conditions, is actually

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.390
be considered pretty good--

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:48.760
that means that in a
utterance with 40 words,

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.400
you'll get four of
them were wrong.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.000
And there is a very
high likelihood

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.163
that those four words--

00:07:53.163 --> 00:07:55.330
some of them will encompass
things that you actually

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:56.470
really care about.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:08:00.197
And the bot will fail, which
will lead the users to be

00:08:00.197 --> 00:08:01.030
a little frustrated.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.740
Because they actually
need to repeat

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.370
stuff that they've
already said before.

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:09.090
So it's not a great
user experience.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.830
And this is even at 90%.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:14.140
But then, imagine
what would happen

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:18.580
if this goes down a little
bit further into 80%, right?

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:21.400
You'll see that in a second.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:26.170
It becomes sort of inoperable.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:29.680
Right, so if you were to say--

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:30.410
a few years ago--

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:34.059
80% or 90% accuracy
for recognizing speech,

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:35.870
people would be pretty happy.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:40.210
But now there's a
higher bar for this.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.610
By the way, this also applies
to Google products, right.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:47.110
So with Google Assistant,
the bar for voice recognition

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.885
is much higher than
it used to be when

00:08:49.885 --> 00:08:51.010
we were doing voice search.

00:08:53.397 --> 00:08:54.730
OK, let's go back to the slides.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:06.480
So the premium models for
better speech recognition--

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.200
the phone one was only available
for people that shared audio.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.930
Now we've made it
available for everyone.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.320
And then for people that
enable data logging--

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:17.730
as an opt-in feature--

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.920
they're now all getting
discounted usage.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.370
So everyone's getting
a benefit, whether they

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:24.767
share data or don't share data.

00:09:24.767 --> 00:09:26.850
We're here to provide the
best service either way.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:31.200
Cool.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.200
Let's talk a little bit
about Text-to-Speech.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.630
So we introduced Cloud
Text-to-Speech that's

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.030
based on WaveNet last year.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:44.630
You can see, here,
a survey we ran

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.660
on the quality of the voices.

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:51.230
And what you see here is
four different voices.

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:57.440
And the left column
shows the standard voice.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:58.940
The middle is the WaveNet.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:00.500
And the right one is human.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:03.600
So if you look at the
yellow one, for example,

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.480
which is the third.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:09.020
The standard is 3.65.

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:12.170
The human is 4.59, you
can see WaveNet is 4.28.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:14.240
So it's really, really close.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:15.680
Let's switch to the demos.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:23.880
So if we go here,
and we type something

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:34.050
like, Hi to the audience at the
session of Google Cloud Next.

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:39.430
And now, let me play
this to you guys with--

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:41.868
this is now standard voice.

00:10:41.868 --> 00:10:42.535
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:10:42.535 --> 00:10:45.972
- Hi to the audience at the
session of Google Cloud Next.

00:10:45.972 --> 00:10:46.555
[END PLAYBACK]

00:10:46.555 --> 00:10:48.400
DAN AHARON: So you can see--

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:52.018
you can hear what he
says very clearly, right?

00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:53.560
You can understand
every single word.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:55.270
But it sounds a little robotic.

00:10:55.270 --> 00:10:55.937
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:10:55.937 --> 00:10:59.132
- Hi to the audience at the
session of Google Cloud Next.

00:10:59.132 --> 00:10:59.715
[END PLAYBACK]

00:10:59.715 --> 00:11:02.918
DAN AHARON: Now, if we switch
to the WaveNet equivalent--

00:11:02.918 --> 00:11:03.585
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:11:03.585 --> 00:11:06.877
- Hi to the audience at the
session of Google Cloud Next.

00:11:06.877 --> 00:11:07.460
[END PLAYBACK]

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:08.120
DAN AHARON: So you can see--

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:09.620
it's worlds apart, right?

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:15.080
And when you use this for
long texts and play it,

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:17.330
I sometimes can't
tell the difference.

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:20.890
It sounds to me like an
NPR News broadcaster.

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:24.890
It's getting pretty
close to what you would

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:27.608
hear on the radio or whatever.

00:11:27.608 --> 00:11:28.400
Back to the slides.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.210
So WaveNet is really cool.

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:40.690
But the problem is it's
hugely compute-intensive.

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:45.070
So in order to generate
a WaveNet sample,

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:47.080
for every single
second of audio,

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:53.200
we need to run 22,000 inferences
on a very heavy neural network.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:56.170
And that was a huge
engineering challenge.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:12:00.590
Originally, it took
a long time to do.

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:03.700
But then our team spent
pretty significant effort,

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:06.210
figuring out how to optimize it.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.110
And the Google team
came up with a way

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:10.150
to do it 1,000 times faster.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:14.200
And this is building on
the infinite computing

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:15.610
power we have with TPUs--

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.130
tensor processing
units-- and other things

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:22.085
that are ML-specialized.

00:12:22.085 --> 00:12:25.580
It lets you run much, much
faster and much better.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:29.748
And we were able to do it at
scale, in a way that it's now

00:12:29.748 --> 00:12:30.790
possible in the industry.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.880
And then, just a
few weeks ago, we

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:39.520
announced a huge
expansion of our voices.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:43.090
So we've now doubled the
number of WaveNet voices.

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:46.400
And if you look back
to April last year,

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.280
we had six WaveNet voices--

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.020
only in English-US.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:52.940
We now have 57 WaveNet voices--

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:56.590
I think in pretty much all of
the languages you see here.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.210
And there's going to be more
coming soon later in the year.

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:00.990
So we have full coverage.

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:05.830
So it's by far more than
anything else, that's

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:08.440
out there-- we're very excited
about WaveNet in the future.

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:15.080
And then to wrap it up, the
other announcement we had

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:18.080
was a bunch of features--

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:19.940
we announced it
went to GA, which

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:21.330
means general availability.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:25.220
That means that they're
covered under our SLA agreement

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:26.840
and are considered
production-ready.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:28.580
So Text-to-Speech--
as a product--

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:32.540
went GA last year.

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:34.550
And then Audio
profiles, which lets

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:38.313
you tune the Text-to-Speech
audio to the medium and a bunch

00:13:38.313 --> 00:13:39.980
of these Speech-to-Text
features-- model

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:43.910
selection, our premium models,
multi-channel recognition,

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:44.570
audio logging.

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:51.610
OK, so to make this
interesting, we

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:55.030
wanted to talk to you a
little bit about new features

00:13:55.030 --> 00:13:57.100
that are about to come soon.

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:00.240
Now this is not
something we typically

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.940
do in all of our
sessions here at Next.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.190
But we wanted to keep it
interesting for you guys

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:09.530
and give you a sense
of what's coming next.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:12.250
The balance of it is
some of these things

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:15.820
might change before you
actually see them officially

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:17.644
launched out in production.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:22.780
So we would love to
still hear your feedback

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:25.840
if you have any ideas
of how to improve these.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:28.570
But we're going to show you
how they look right now.

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:34.640
So we're going to talk about
the two main groups of use cases

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.240
that we serve with
speech recognition.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.990
So the first is
human-computer interaction.

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:43.370
That's kind of the traditional
investment of speech at Google.

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:45.810
And then the second is
human-to-human interaction.

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:50.207
So you could think
about analyzing

00:14:50.207 --> 00:14:52.040
phone calls between a
customer and an agent,

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.190
or analyzing meetings,
or things like that--

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:57.780
where you're talking about
speech between multiple people.

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:00.140
So we're going to
have new developments

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:04.070
for each of these two to
talk about a little bit.

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:05.570
Let's start with the first one.

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:10.570
So we've had something called
phrase hints in our API

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.460
for a long while now.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:16.413
The problem is it's really
hard to get it right, right?

00:15:16.413 --> 00:15:18.580
Sorry, actually, let me
first describe what it does.

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:20.747
What phrase hints does is
it allows you to customize

00:15:20.747 --> 00:15:23.360
the API to specific phrases.

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.512
So if you have specific product
names, or company names,

00:15:26.512 --> 00:15:27.970
or other things
that you're looking

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.190
for that you expect
users might say,

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:33.340
you can add that to the
recognition command.

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:36.430
And then it will improve
recognition quality.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:41.170
The trouble is it could also
increase false positives.

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:44.350
If you add a string, it might
start recognizing that string

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:46.720
even if it wasn't said.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:51.370
So by default, it's actually--

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:53.410
it's set to be pretty weak.

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:54.250
So that's a problem.

00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:57.190
The other problem is
you need to come up

00:15:57.190 --> 00:16:01.990
with different phrases for
everything you want to send.

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:06.730
So we're going to come out
with a few features that

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:08.170
address this.

00:16:08.170 --> 00:16:11.260
First is what we call strength.

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:13.085
So as you can see
in blue over there,

00:16:13.085 --> 00:16:14.710
we're going to give
you the opportunity

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:17.360
to set the strength for
each speech context.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:20.680
And so you can set it
much stronger than what

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:23.120
we made available up until now.

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.700
And it makes it
much more useful.

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:29.510
You'll be able to see that
in a demo in a few minutes.

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:30.860
The second is classes.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:35.103
So for things like
month, for example.

00:16:35.103 --> 00:16:37.270
If you just tell us that
you're looking for a month,

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:39.005
we can figure out
what's behind it--

00:16:39.005 --> 00:16:41.380
you don't need to spell out
all of the different months--

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:43.910
January and February, et cetera.

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:46.690
So here's an example
list of classes.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:50.480
And we'll share the final
one when this is ready to go.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:53.890
But these can help you with your
different recognition tasks.

00:16:57.140 --> 00:16:59.475
OK, let's switch to the demo.

00:17:12.099 --> 00:17:13.200
OK, here it is.

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:15.960
So here-- just as an example--

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:21.980
there's a sequence of
digits that we recorded.

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:23.623
Let me play it to you.

00:17:23.623 --> 00:17:24.290
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.369
- 31378654.

00:17:26.369 --> 00:17:28.040
[END PLAYBACK]

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.730
DAN AHARON: So you can
kind of understand what

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:33.170
I'm saying in this recording.

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:35.483
Then if you look
at this other one,

00:17:35.483 --> 00:17:36.650
you can see it's very noisy.

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:40.895
So it's actually really hard
to tell what the digits are.

00:17:40.895 --> 00:17:41.562
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:17:41.562 --> 00:17:44.092
- 31378654.

00:17:44.092 --> 00:17:45.505
[END PLAYBACK]

00:17:45.505 --> 00:17:49.400
DAN AHARON: OK, so if you
take the original one--

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:50.930
let me just change this.

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:57.390
If you take the original one,
and you send it to the API,

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:07.975
then we'll see it recognizes it
properly, with 100% accuracy.

00:18:07.975 --> 00:18:10.600
Recognizing a digit sequence is
pretty hard, by the way, right?

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:13.990
Because even if you
get one of them wrong,

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:16.000
the whole string is wrong.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:20.620
So in a way, it's
like a full sentence.

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:22.680
Now let's try the
same on the noisy one.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:28.670
So I just sent a cURL
command, like you see here.

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:31.310
And you can see, it
recognizes incorrectly.

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:35.020
So it says, we went three
78654, because it doesn't really

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:39.100
know that it should expect
a sequence of digits.

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:40.600
So here, what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:44.120
going to use the class
operand, which means a digit--

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:46.630
I'm going to tell it it
should expect a sequence

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:50.467
of eight consecutive digits.

00:18:50.467 --> 00:18:51.550
I'm going to send it over.

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:58.090
And you can see, it still
didn't change the result.

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:03.910
The reason is it's still using
relatively weak speech context.

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:05.760
So now, when I also
add strength high--

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:08.010
like you can see here in blue--

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:09.090
we can try it again.

00:19:12.910 --> 00:19:15.750
And you can see, now it
actually recognizes it

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:19.680
with 100% accuracy.

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:24.890
So that's speech context
strength and classes, guy's.

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:29.060
So just as a disclaimer--

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:30.810
it doesn't always work right.

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:33.140
Speech recognition is a
little bit of a science

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:34.265
and a little bit of an art.

00:19:34.265 --> 00:19:37.060
There's going to
be cases where you

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:40.910
want to force it to
recognize digits,

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:42.470
but it'll recognize
something else.

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:44.920
And there's going to be
cases where it recognizes

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:45.860
it too strong, right?

00:19:45.860 --> 00:19:50.090
So it falsely recognizes digits
when you don't want it to.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:53.750
So these are things that allow
you a little bit more control

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:55.940
to play with the API and
reach the settings that

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:57.000
are optimal for you.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:59.322
So for each use case, it
might be a little different.

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:09.540
OK, let's talk about the
second major use case, which

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:14.220
is human-to-human interaction.

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:20.070
And there, we've had streaming
for a long period of time,

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:22.680
but streaming is
limited to one minute.

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:26.730
We do have LongRunningRecognize
that could use any length

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:31.530
that you want, but it
doesn't work in real time.

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:33.780
The problem of speech
analytics usually

00:20:33.780 --> 00:20:36.160
requires long audio files.

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:38.040
So if it's a phone
call recording,

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:39.700
it could be five
minutes or 10 minutes.

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:41.617
If it's a meeting, it
could be multiple hours.

00:20:41.617 --> 00:20:44.710
If it's a movie could
be multiple hours.

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:47.280
So what we're coming out
with is a new feature,

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:49.120
called ResultEndTime.

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:52.590
And what it does-- it tells
you how far the recognition

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:53.910
happened so far.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.250
And then you can
restart a new session

00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:58.050
from where the last one ended.

00:20:58.050 --> 00:21:00.660
So you can restart a
session whenever you want

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:04.950
and still get 100%
of the same results

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:08.260
as if you were going more.

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:11.190
So let me show you
how this actually

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:12.570
looks like in real life.

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:19.320
OK, so what you see
here in this demo--

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:23.110
I can make this
maybe a bit bigger.

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:26.503
Here, we asked it to
restart every 10 seconds.

00:21:26.503 --> 00:21:28.170
So every 10 seconds,
it basically closes

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:29.910
the streaming connection
and starts a new streaming

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:30.410
connection.

00:21:30.410 --> 00:21:35.050
So right now, the API
supports up to 60 seconds.

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:38.400
So if I didn't use any timer,
it would just go up until 60.

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:40.650
But in order to make the
demo more interesting,

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:43.960
we set it to 10
to make it easier.

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:45.390
I'm going to click Start.

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:48.570
And now you can see--
it's listening to me.

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.265
And it's showing
these red boxes.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:54.920
So the red box--

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:58.350
you can see-- is turning
into green once it

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:02.350
detects an end of an utterance.

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:07.580
Oh, it's not a good combination
with me talking in the echo--

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:08.270
I see.

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:18.960
But you can see it's
turning into green once it

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:23.300
detects an end of utterance.

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:25.120
And you can--

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:25.620
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:28.760
--yeah, that's nice.

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:34.510
The yellow boxes show
where it's restarting.

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:41.840
And so you can see
the actual audio

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:44.390
is not impacted by
when it restarts.

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:47.890
If I keep on
talking now, you can

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:50.200
see that it keeps
on the recognition,

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:52.660
even though it's a whole
new streaming session.

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:56.155
Right, it keeps on with all
of the same data that it had.

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:04.020
So you can see it
carries over, right?

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:08.160
So with this method, you can
basically do endless streaming

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:11.830
for as long as you want.

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:16.620
Let me show you how that looks
like in the command line mode.

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:21.985
So I'm going to talk now.

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:27.610
And now I'm going to talk again.

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:31.110
And let's see--

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:32.670
OK, you can see
it did a restart.

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:34.500
And it didn't impact
the recognition.

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:38.460
By the way, I was standing--
at the mic is on my laptop.

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:42.430
I was standing a little
further out from the laptop.

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:45.490
So that probably reduces
results a little bit.

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:51.900
Wow, that was a really
long, utterance.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:55.270
OK, so thank you.

00:23:59.050 --> 00:24:03.182
So that's endless streaming.

00:24:03.182 --> 00:24:05.140
How many developers do
we have in the audience?

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:11.330
Who wants to see the code
that's powering this demo?

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:14.950
OK, a lot of hands up there.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:16.460
OK, let's do it.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:19.855
The only caveat is I
didn't write this code,

00:24:19.855 --> 00:24:21.620
so I'll do my best
to give it justice.

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:28.700
But please forgive
me if I don't do it.

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:32.432
So this is then code in Node.js.

00:24:32.432 --> 00:24:33.140
Can you guys see?

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:36.350
Or should I make it bigger?

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:38.040
Is that better?

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:40.550
OK, let me scroll
down to the bottom,

00:24:40.550 --> 00:24:44.130
because that's
where the action is.

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:47.440
So here in the
bottom, you can see

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:51.390
what it does is it
starts a record session.

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:52.900
And they use a
program called rec,

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:55.660
which is from the SoX family.

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:57.820
And it uses that to
capture audio from the mic.

00:24:57.820 --> 00:25:01.190
And then it sends it into
audio stream transform,

00:25:01.190 --> 00:25:03.490
which is a transform
stream, something

00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:05.470
that's readable and writeable.

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:09.320
And then, it calls something
called startStream().

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:10.900
So let's scroll up
to startStream().

00:25:14.950 --> 00:25:18.060
And what startStream() does
is it starts in audio array.

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:21.810
And then it starts a new
streamingRecognize session.

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:24.780
So this just calls the
Speech-to-Text API.

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:27.420
And then it sends
all the data that's

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:30.570
coming in to speechCallback.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:31.990
And then, here,
you set a timeout,

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:33.150
so you restart the stream.

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:37.280
This is the 10 seconds
that we had in our demo.

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:39.660
So let's take a look
at speechCallback.

00:25:39.660 --> 00:25:44.010
What speechCallback does--
it gets the resultEndTime

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:44.710
from the API.

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:47.410
This is what we
talked about earlier.

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:50.110
And then it calculates
the correct time.

00:25:50.110 --> 00:25:57.000
So here in the demo,
you can see there's

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.430
a corrected time, which is--

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:02.220
you need to know that it's 60
seconds from when we started

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:06.570
the demo, not just the offset
from where we currently

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.700
are in the current array.

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:14.580
So it calculates that
and prints it out.

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:18.900
And then it looks
for an isFinal.

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:21.330
If it gets isFinal,
that's the flag

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:23.950
that says the end of
utterance was detected.

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:26.190
So you can mark it as green.

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:30.090
Or otherwise, it keeps
using the same audio buffer

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:32.310
and then marks it as red.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:41.460
Then, let's look
at the callback--

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:44.980
that's another
interesting important one.

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:48.150
So here, what it
does is it calculates

00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:49.450
what we call chunkTime.

00:26:49.450 --> 00:26:55.060
It's basically the size
of an audio array element.

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:58.260
So you take the full
streaming limit,

00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:00.720
divide by the length
of the audio array,

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:02.850
and then you get what is
the size of each element

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:05.170
in the array-- or a chunk.

00:27:05.170 --> 00:27:14.520
And then with that
chunk, you calculate

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:18.660
how many chunks you need to
grab from the previous array

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:23.280
and how many chunks you need to
grab from the current, array--

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:25.690
so lastAudioArray.length.

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:30.970
This tells you-- it's like here.

00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:33.970
You can see-- you
need this part, right?

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:36.280
When you go, after
the yellow part--

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:38.230
this part-- you need
to grab the audio that

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:39.760
came before the yellow.

00:27:39.760 --> 00:27:41.680
So that's what's
being done here.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:44.770
You take it from lastAudioArray.

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:52.980
And then you recognize it.

00:27:52.980 --> 00:27:58.230
And then, the last important
thing to watch out for

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:00.330
is this restart function.

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:04.640
So it's really important
to run removeListener().

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:06.030
If you don't do
that, then you'll

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.905
have two listeners
running in the background.

00:28:07.905 --> 00:28:09.450
So every time you
restart a stream,

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:13.990
you need to remove the listener
and not just set it to null.

00:28:13.990 --> 00:28:14.860
And that's it.

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:17.830
There's a restart counter,
so it knows how many times

00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:20.630
it did 10 seconds.

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:24.180
And that's it.

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:25.740
So that's endless
streaming, guys.

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:31.680
Thanks.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:42.010
So just to talk a little bit
about limitations with the demo

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:42.700
you just saw.

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:46.180
So it handles two arrays--

00:28:46.180 --> 00:28:48.340
two audio arrays.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:51.383
And we set each
one to 10 seconds.

00:28:51.383 --> 00:28:53.050
You can set them for
longer if you want.

00:28:53.050 --> 00:28:56.715
But in this demo, if you talk
more than 20 seconds at a time,

00:28:56.715 --> 00:28:58.090
it won't be able
to capture that,

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:00.865
because it's only set up to
take to capture 20 seconds.

00:29:03.710 --> 00:29:08.320
The other thing is that it does
arithmetic on the audio array,

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:11.860
based on the length of
time that has passed.

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:14.530
So it works well for
a constant bit rate.

00:29:14.530 --> 00:29:16.060
So LINEAR16 is fine.

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:18.158
Mulaw is also fine-- even
though it's compressed,

00:29:18.158 --> 00:29:19.450
it's still a constant bit rate.

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:23.440
But if you try MP3 and stuff
like that, it won't work.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:27.130
Luckily, there is very
little real-time streaming

00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:28.970
that people do with MP3, right?

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:33.260
Because you'd have to compress
it in real-time as well.

00:29:33.260 --> 00:29:36.640
And the other thing to
note-- diarization context

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:38.000
is actually important.

00:29:38.000 --> 00:29:40.875
And if you restart a stream,
it resets that context.

00:29:40.875 --> 00:29:42.250
So if you want to
do diarization,

00:29:42.250 --> 00:29:46.280
this will impact those results.

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:49.030
The other thing is
this is available here

00:29:49.030 --> 00:29:51.850
in the Node.js client library.

00:29:51.850 --> 00:29:54.220
We're going to add it to
all of our client libraries

00:29:54.220 --> 00:29:56.570
eventually, but they're
not all available yet.

00:29:56.570 --> 00:30:00.837
So this is something
that you'll need to check

00:30:00.837 --> 00:30:01.920
before you start using it.

00:30:05.462 --> 00:30:06.730
Cool.

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:12.580
So let's transition
to talk a little bit

00:30:12.580 --> 00:30:16.610
about speech analytics.

00:30:16.610 --> 00:30:18.070
So there's a lot
of cool things you

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:24.580
could do with speech analytics,
especially in call centers--

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:26.260
so understanding
customer sentiment,

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:29.960
understanding why
users are calling.

00:30:29.960 --> 00:30:35.920
Figuring out how to reduce
call volume or how to upsell--

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:38.230
like if a customer is
asking for a product that's

00:30:38.230 --> 00:30:41.230
out of inventory or stuff like
that and you want to follow up.

00:30:41.230 --> 00:30:44.810
There's like all these cool
analyses you want to do.

00:30:44.810 --> 00:30:50.020
But it's actually
non-trivial to set it up.

00:30:50.020 --> 00:30:51.910
Actually, could we switch
to the other slide?

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:55.800
Let me go forward.

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:01.390
So there's a lot of
technical steps involved,

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:04.940
and very typically, when
we talk to customers,

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:07.733
they love our technology--
they love our AI.

00:31:07.733 --> 00:31:09.650
But in order to actually
take advantage of it,

00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:11.472
they need to go to
the IT department

00:31:11.472 --> 00:31:13.180
and ask them to build
something for them.

00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:17.800
Or they need to an SI to build
a visualization platform.

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:21.470
Just having access to
the API is not enough.

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:23.770
So we want to help with that.

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:27.370
And a way to do that is with
what we are calling the speech

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:29.702
analytics framework.

00:31:29.702 --> 00:31:31.660
So this is kind of like
a design pattern of how

00:31:31.660 --> 00:31:35.170
to do analysis on speech.

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:37.217
And we're showing an
example of how it could

00:31:37.217 --> 00:31:38.300
apply for the call center.

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:42.410
But you could also apply
it in any other use case.

00:31:42.410 --> 00:31:47.620
So you can see the audio comes
into Google's Cloud Storage.

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:50.410
And then it gets processed
with Cloud Functions.

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:53.950
Cloud Functions then uses
are different AI tools--

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:57.730
Speech API, Natural
Language API, AutoML--

00:31:57.730 --> 00:32:00.880
to understand what's actually
in those audio files.

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:03.370
And then that data gets sent--

00:32:03.370 --> 00:32:07.300
through Cloud Pub/Sub and
DataFlow-- into BigQuery.

00:32:07.300 --> 00:32:09.660
DataFlow handles the
streaming information,

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:12.280
and then it gets
stored in BigQuery,

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:13.400
which can do the analysis.

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.700
It can help us run queries
and better understand

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:18.970
what's in those audio files.

00:32:18.970 --> 00:32:22.570
And then we have a
visualization layer.

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:24.580
The one we're showing
here was built in React,

00:32:24.580 --> 00:32:28.930
but you could build whatever you
want in whatever tool you want.

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:32.800
And Identity Aware
Proxy for security,

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:37.130
which leads you to the final
result in the visualization.

00:32:37.130 --> 00:32:39.140
And you're going to see
a demo and a little bit.

00:32:39.140 --> 00:32:42.610
It's pretty cool.

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:45.910
What we want is for
any cloud customer

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:49.390
to be able to get from
having audio files to this

00:32:49.390 --> 00:32:51.310
in two weeks.

00:32:51.310 --> 00:32:52.810
And we're setting
up a program where

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:59.290
you can ask for help with SIs or
whatever to help you get there.

00:32:59.290 --> 00:33:01.850
So to elaborate a little
bit more about that,

00:33:01.850 --> 00:33:07.110
let me invite Kevin-- he's
going to talk lots more.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:08.990
KEVIN NORTHOVER: All right.

00:33:08.990 --> 00:33:10.070
Well, thank you, Dan.

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:12.980
It's great to be here and to be
able to talk to you about what

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:15.530
we've been doing at Delta
Dental with the tools

00:33:15.530 --> 00:33:18.620
Dan has been telling us about.

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:21.020
Good, yeah, getting
those back up.

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:24.090
All right.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:26.700
So we're a health
insurance payer.

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:29.910
That means we have two
large communities of users--

00:33:29.910 --> 00:33:33.070
the members, like you or I,
who get dental treatment.

00:33:33.070 --> 00:33:34.770
And it's covered
by a plan that's

00:33:34.770 --> 00:33:37.770
either employer-funded or
increasingly one that we

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:40.470
bought ourselves on
the individual market.

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:43.380
And the dentists themselves.

00:33:43.380 --> 00:33:46.440
We cover about 1.7
million members.

00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:48.000
The bulk of those,
obviously, are

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:49.470
in New Jersey and Connecticut.

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:51.460
But we cover people
all over the country.

00:33:51.460 --> 00:33:54.970
For example, we have 100,000
members here in California.

00:33:54.970 --> 00:33:57.870
And we're part of a larger
network of Delta Dental

00:33:57.870 --> 00:34:00.540
companies-- there are
nearly 30 in all, which

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:03.330
cover 73 million Americans.

00:34:03.330 --> 00:34:05.490
And the association
also provides

00:34:05.490 --> 00:34:07.440
some central, national services.

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:10.110
The obvious one is
simply a directory

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:14.699
of practicing dentists that
we all need to process claims.

00:34:14.699 --> 00:34:19.260
So why the call center?

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:22.170
Somebody said in the
keynote this morning--

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:25.050
the call center is still
really the primary point

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:28.230
of contact for many
companies for many of users.

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:31.469
And it's particularly true for
us with members and dentists.

00:34:31.469 --> 00:34:32.550
We do a lot of calls--

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:35.230
about 4,300 hours a month.

00:34:35.230 --> 00:34:37.549
That's about 4,000 calls a day.

00:34:37.549 --> 00:34:38.580
All right?

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:42.179
And 3/4 of our calls come
from dentists' offices.

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:44.580
And usually those are
questions about benefits

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:46.500
eligibility-- does
this person who's just

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:48.320
walked in the door
have insurance?

00:34:48.320 --> 00:34:50.780
Has their insurance expired?

00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:54.820
Or it can get down
to the procedures.

00:34:54.820 --> 00:34:57.090
Is their children's
retainer covered?

00:34:57.090 --> 00:34:59.430
When can I get my
crown replaced?

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:01.097
All of these kinds
of questions come up.

00:35:01.097 --> 00:35:03.138
In other words, what's
the coverage in the plans.

00:35:03.138 --> 00:35:05.880
And as we all know-- from our
medical and dental experience--

00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:07.790
plans vary.

00:35:07.790 --> 00:35:11.510
So that's one thing we know from
talking to the customer center.

00:35:11.510 --> 00:35:14.600
The other thing we know-- from
looking at our phone logs--

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:17.190
is that most of our
calls are short.

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:19.910
That spike you see in the
histogram on the right

00:35:19.910 --> 00:35:21.620
is centered on 80 seconds.

00:35:21.620 --> 00:35:24.770
It's actually 65
to 95 seconds wide.

00:35:24.770 --> 00:35:27.020
And the interesting
thing about that

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:28.790
is that in that length
of time, you only

00:35:28.790 --> 00:35:31.490
have time to cover one subject.

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:35.420
So from the point of view of
someone doing a data science

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:37.770
and modeling analysis,
that's a very nice,

00:35:37.770 --> 00:35:39.740
clean data set to work with.

00:35:39.740 --> 00:35:42.620
And the analytics results
I'm going to show you--

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:45.020
so far-- are all based
on analyzing what's

00:35:45.020 --> 00:35:46.160
going on there in detail.

00:35:46.160 --> 00:35:48.980
We figured if we understand
single subjects well,

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:51.320
we'll be able to get into
the more complicated subjects

00:35:51.320 --> 00:35:54.150
in the longer course.

00:35:54.150 --> 00:35:56.510
So the first thing we did
was take the Speech-to-Text

00:35:56.510 --> 00:35:59.420
from Dan's tools and run it
through the natural language

00:35:59.420 --> 00:36:01.010
processing.

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:03.350
And natural language--
the sentiment analysis

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:07.190
will give you back a sentiment
score for every sentence

00:36:07.190 --> 00:36:10.170
in the transcript.

00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:12.390
And if you really want
to test Dan's stuff,

00:36:12.390 --> 00:36:15.510
try saying that sentence fast.

00:36:15.510 --> 00:36:17.310
Ha.

00:36:17.310 --> 00:36:18.870
OK.

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:21.330
And what we then did was
marry that back to the word

00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:24.990
timings that we get from
the Speech-to-Text and plots

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:29.130
the distribution of sentiment
as it tracks through a call.

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:33.360
What you can see is that
these are not movie reviews.

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:36.810
There's no one number that
characterizes the call.

00:36:36.810 --> 00:36:39.210
What we have, though,
is a great tool

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:42.750
for pulling apart the
structure of a conversation.

00:36:42.750 --> 00:36:44.610
And we can see all
the patterns that you

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:47.910
would like to see in any kind
of customer service call show

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:50.220
up immediately in the sentiment.

00:36:50.220 --> 00:36:52.890
Right, but it's not
very useful for getting

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:54.630
quantitative results.

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:55.380
So what do you do?

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:58.750
You have to get a
bit more subtle.

00:36:58.750 --> 00:37:01.350
What we did was split the
calls out by type of caller.

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:04.020
So look at the top
two rows first.

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:07.115
In the left column, we have
dental offices and members

00:37:07.115 --> 00:37:09.200
that we talked about earlier.

00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:12.510
We have the same sentiment
plots for each of those--

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:15.660
these ones are actually
three month averages.

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:19.020
On the right hand side,
which we plot the residuals--

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:21.780
we subtracted the
average sentiment profile

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:23.707
from the individual classes.

00:37:23.707 --> 00:37:25.290
And you can see,
now, we actually have

00:37:25.290 --> 00:37:26.820
something that we can model.

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:29.685
We can fit a straight
line to this data.

00:37:29.685 --> 00:37:30.810
And it's a pretty good fit.

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:35.690
The pale blue bars and
95% confidence limits.

00:37:35.690 --> 00:37:37.170
So two things are obvious.

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:40.050
One is that members and
dentists are different.

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:41.850
Either dentists
are a gloomy lot,

00:37:41.850 --> 00:37:43.830
or we're continually
giving them bad news

00:37:43.830 --> 00:37:45.810
about how much of
the procedure they

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:47.353
want to do is actually covered.

00:37:47.353 --> 00:37:49.020
But we're doing quite
well with members,

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:52.680
and the sentiment is generally
rising through the call.

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:57.240
The bottom row-- it turns out
that some of our major accounts

00:37:57.240 --> 00:37:59.340
have a specific
phone line for them.

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:00.970
So we can identify
a major account.

00:38:00.970 --> 00:38:05.400
And we give the same number to
the dentists and the members.

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:07.740
So you'd expect those calls
to split the difference.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:09.900
And they do, very nicely.

00:38:09.900 --> 00:38:13.140
So what this is telling us
is not necessarily anything

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:14.850
we can immediately use directly.

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:17.820
But it's telling us that there
is real structure in the calls.

00:38:17.820 --> 00:38:19.800
And it is worth analyzing
them in more depth.

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:20.940
And we're starting to do.

00:38:20.940 --> 00:38:23.160
One of the things
we've started recently

00:38:23.160 --> 00:38:26.190
is we've turned on the
premium phone model.

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:28.230
And we're now running
speaker diarization.

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:30.090
And so the next
generation of these plots

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:31.830
will actually separate
out the sentiment

00:38:31.830 --> 00:38:34.140
profile for the caller
and the sentiment

00:38:34.140 --> 00:38:36.450
profile for the agent.

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:40.980
Talking of agents, you can
do the same thing for agents.

00:38:40.980 --> 00:38:44.190
The first time I showed
this plot to our customer

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:49.410
service team leaders, I hadn't
anonymized the agent names.

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:50.970
And the immediate
reaction of one

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:52.680
of the team leaders
to the top one

00:38:52.680 --> 00:38:55.870
was, she's no longer
with the company.

00:38:55.870 --> 00:38:56.850
Right.

00:38:56.850 --> 00:39:00.740
The nice thing about that
is that was a huge win.

00:39:00.740 --> 00:39:02.480
Because it gives you
instant credibility

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:05.310
with the business user that
the kind of measurements

00:39:05.310 --> 00:39:08.750
you're making on this data--
that seem to be very abstract--

00:39:08.750 --> 00:39:10.220
are actually
measuring real things

00:39:10.220 --> 00:39:13.530
they're seeing in dealing
with their employees.

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:16.990
So, nice one.

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:18.830
The other, next
metric we looked at

00:39:18.830 --> 00:39:21.640
was something that isn't there--

00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:23.710
the dead air between calls.

00:39:23.710 --> 00:39:26.063
All conversation has
natural rhythms in it

00:39:26.063 --> 00:39:26.980
and there are pauses--

00:39:26.980 --> 00:39:28.670
they vary in length.

00:39:28.670 --> 00:39:30.970
This is a standard metric
in call centers-- obviously,

00:39:30.970 --> 00:39:33.600
you want your people talking.

00:39:33.600 --> 00:39:36.160
We got asked to do this,
by the call center,

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:39.850
because they had an
agent who they'd noticed

00:39:39.850 --> 00:39:42.860
had a lot of dead air-- a lot
of pauses in the conversation.

00:39:42.860 --> 00:39:45.472
And so you can see that
in the plot on the right.

00:39:45.472 --> 00:39:46.930
The other thing
you can also see is

00:39:46.930 --> 00:39:50.190
that the call structure shows
up in exactly the same way

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:51.580
in the dead air--

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:54.140
the familiar phrases,
the opening greeting,

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:57.730
and the end of the call,
we don't pause very much.

00:39:57.730 --> 00:40:01.560
When we're thinking about
working an issue, we do.

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:04.080
This has been an enormously
valuable metric for us.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:08.400
We've used it in customer
service for coaching agents.

00:40:08.400 --> 00:40:10.800
We have done tests,
instrumenting

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:15.090
the agent's desktop, and been
able to show that our agents--

00:40:15.090 --> 00:40:17.580
in the long pauses, when
they're working an issue--

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:21.433
are looking at applications
like sticky notes and Outlook.

00:40:21.433 --> 00:40:23.850
They're looking up information
in their personal knowledge

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:24.960
repositories.

00:40:24.960 --> 00:40:28.540
That tells us what we have
to build next for them.

00:40:28.540 --> 00:40:32.820
We also instrumented it to test
whether dual monitors would

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:34.920
improve-- so we
can quantitatively

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:37.500
see impacts in the
customer center

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:39.972
just from these very
simple measurements.

00:40:39.972 --> 00:40:41.430
And that's one of
the points that I

00:40:41.430 --> 00:40:44.460
hope comes out of this--
that once you have

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:46.660
all the sophisticated
technology of speech

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:48.270
detection, natural
language, you're

00:40:48.270 --> 00:40:51.420
able to ask very simple
business-oriented questions

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:52.520
and get good answers.

00:40:55.960 --> 00:40:59.160
Another metric-- we've
worked on topic modeling,

00:40:59.160 --> 00:41:01.280
but we haven't yet got
any very good results.

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:03.613
And we're about to go through
another iteration of that.

00:41:03.613 --> 00:41:05.610
Topic modeling, for
us, is really hard.

00:41:05.610 --> 00:41:07.710
Because-- you saw-- we
had benefits eligibility

00:41:07.710 --> 00:41:08.820
earlier on.

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:10.290
That's a huge subject.

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:13.530
We need to be able to get to the
second tier and do it reliably.

00:41:13.530 --> 00:41:16.750
But there are some things you
can do just by counting words.

00:41:16.750 --> 00:41:18.470
In this particular
case, we had a change

00:41:18.470 --> 00:41:20.387
that had been talked
about in the organization

00:41:20.387 --> 00:41:21.510
for a long time.

00:41:21.510 --> 00:41:23.040
We wanted to change
some procedures.

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:24.920
We could never make
the business case.

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:27.270
By just counting
those two words--

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:29.490
across a sample of transcripts--

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:31.380
we were able to
make the business

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:33.515
case to change the IVR system.

00:41:33.515 --> 00:41:35.640
That change will roll out
at the end of this month.

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:40.470
We expect that to cut the number
of calls we have by about 20%.

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:43.720
In a year, that will pay for
our investment in this project.

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:47.142
Now, this situation may
not apply in your business,

00:41:47.142 --> 00:41:49.350
but we're pretty sure-- from
what we've been finding,

00:41:49.350 --> 00:41:51.300
looking at the
transcripts in depth--

00:41:51.300 --> 00:41:53.850
that everybody has some
kind of problem like this.

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:58.200
There's something there, but
it's buried at the moment.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:01.920
And the last one-- tying
back to IVR systems again--

00:42:01.920 --> 00:42:06.760
is how many people here like
going through long IVR menus?

00:42:06.760 --> 00:42:07.450
Right.

00:42:07.450 --> 00:42:11.270
Well, it shows up when you talk
to the agent at the end of it.

00:42:11.270 --> 00:42:12.810
And we can measure that.

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:15.310
And we tied back the
sentiment scores--

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:18.820
and particularly the negative
sentences in the call--

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:22.190
to the path through
the IVR system.

00:42:22.190 --> 00:42:25.550
So keep them short.

00:42:25.550 --> 00:42:27.830
OK, that's some of
the examples analytics

00:42:27.830 --> 00:42:29.360
we've been able to
do on this data.

00:42:29.360 --> 00:42:32.690
Now I'd like to go a
little bit technical

00:42:32.690 --> 00:42:35.840
and talk about
how we built this.

00:42:35.840 --> 00:42:38.420
We built it in a framework
that's very like the one

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.160
that Dan just outlined to you.

00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:42.630
We upload our recordings
into the cloud.

00:42:42.630 --> 00:42:44.330
And then when you
upload the recording

00:42:44.330 --> 00:42:47.340
to the storage bucket, it
triggers a Cloud Function.

00:42:47.340 --> 00:42:50.030
This entirely
serverless pipeline--

00:42:50.030 --> 00:42:52.770
it's scalable, horizontally
and vertically.

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:56.060
Once that Cloud Function
finishes processing,

00:42:56.060 --> 00:42:58.760
the transcribe interface
writes the output back,

00:42:58.760 --> 00:43:02.000
as the transcript record,
into the same storage bucket.

00:43:02.000 --> 00:43:04.520
It triggers another instance
of the Cloud Function.

00:43:04.520 --> 00:43:07.130
And we just cascade
through those instances

00:43:07.130 --> 00:43:09.220
until we've got all
our processing done.

00:43:09.220 --> 00:43:10.580
All right.

00:43:10.580 --> 00:43:13.370
We've found that this is a
very reliable way of doing it.

00:43:13.370 --> 00:43:15.650
It's straightforward to deploy.

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:16.910
It runs very well.

00:43:16.910 --> 00:43:19.400
We can scale it vertically.

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:21.770
And it scales very well
with increasing volumes

00:43:21.770 --> 00:43:23.030
in a single project.

00:43:23.030 --> 00:43:24.620
It's also easy to
scale horizontally.

00:43:24.620 --> 00:43:26.430
If we've got a slightly
different problem

00:43:26.430 --> 00:43:29.420
we want to work on or we want to
process a data set for somebody

00:43:29.420 --> 00:43:32.050
else, we just spin
up another project,

00:43:32.050 --> 00:43:35.210
install all the
software, and go.

00:43:35.210 --> 00:43:37.210
And because it's all
Cloud Function-based,

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:38.600
it's a quick deployment.

00:43:38.600 --> 00:43:40.730
We can spin up a
project in three hours.

00:43:40.730 --> 00:43:42.980
And it only takes that long
because I haven't finished

00:43:42.980 --> 00:43:46.840
writing the automation script.

00:43:46.840 --> 00:43:49.980
So again-- a little bit
about how we build this.

00:43:49.980 --> 00:43:51.120
We develop in Python.

00:43:51.120 --> 00:43:55.290
We wrap all the Google APIs
in our own Python classes.

00:43:55.290 --> 00:43:57.750
And we use those
exclusively for the cloud

00:43:57.750 --> 00:44:00.060
and the on-premise applications.

00:44:00.060 --> 00:44:01.920
This really reduces
the amount of effort

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:04.740
we have to put in on building
out a new application to handle

00:44:04.740 --> 00:44:07.800
configuration
exceptions, retries,

00:44:07.800 --> 00:44:09.490
all those kinds of things.

00:44:09.490 --> 00:44:12.150
It also turns out to be
really essential for handling

00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:13.470
the results.

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:15.540
Speech-to-Text gives
the results back

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:18.180
in a very natural format
for Speech-to-Text--

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:21.120
their word timings
and their words.

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:23.970
Natural Language gives us
back the sentiment analysis

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:25.350
in the natural format.

00:44:25.350 --> 00:44:27.120
It's sentences in
character offset,

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.730
because when they built that,
they didn't have word timings

00:44:29.730 --> 00:44:30.810
information.

00:44:30.810 --> 00:44:33.210
We have to cross-reference
all of those.

00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:35.850
And we've found this as we've
looked into other Google APIs--

00:44:35.850 --> 00:44:39.010
that there's a lot of work in
just integrating all of this

00:44:39.010 --> 00:44:39.510
together.

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:43.960
OK.

00:44:43.960 --> 00:44:46.650
What I'd like to do now
is run a short demo.

00:44:46.650 --> 00:44:48.790
Dan talked about
the visualizations.

00:44:55.010 --> 00:44:57.816
OK, if you could cut
